      "text": "To determine the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on two 6-sided dice is a prime number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify all possible outcomes**: Each die has 6 faces, so there are \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible combinations when two dice are tossed.\n\n2. **List all possible sums**: The possible sums range from \\(2\\) (1+1) to \\(12\\) (6+6).\n\n3. **Identify which of these sums are prime numbers**: The prime numbers between 2 and 12 are: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11. Note that 2 and 12 (minuend and subtrahend of the sum not relevant for 2d6) are not sums of prime numbers in the context of 3d6 with 6s caps and removal of repetition/excess; only numbers from 1 to 6 in resolution context are really considered + one-number extended range. Avoiding redundancy, note identifying \\(\\{ 2, 3, 5, 7, 11\\}\\) actual solution possibilities.\n\n4. **Count the number of ways to achieve each prime sum**: Take the prime sums' quartet mentioned above.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Sum of } 2: &1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\n&\\text{Sum of } 3: &1 + 2, 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\n&\\text{Sum of } 5: &1 + 4, 2 + 3, 3 + 2, 4 + 1\\\\\n&\\text{Sum of } 7: &1+6, 2+5, 3+4, 4+3, 5+2, 6+1\\\\\n&\\text{Sum of } 11: &5+6, 6+5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n5. **Count successful outcomes:**\n    - \\(2\\): 1 combination\n    - \\(3\\): 2 combinations\n    - \\(5\\): 4 combinations\n    - \\(7\\): 6 combinations\n    - \\(11\\): 2 combinations\n\nOverall, there are \\(1 + 2 + 4 + 6 + 2 = 15\\) ways to get a prime sum out of 36 possible outcomes. Thus, the desired probability is \\(\\frac{15}{36}\\). \n\n6. **Simplify the fraction**: \n\nReduce: $$ \\frac{15}{36} = \\frac{5}{12}. $$\n\nThus, the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on the two dice is a prime number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{12}}\\).",
